---
title: Custom Marks and Renderers
description: Extend the grammar with typed mark channels and keyed scene nodes, or replace the mounted renderer without bypassing chart semantics.
---
NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use a custom mark when a visualization fits the shared scene model but is not\nexpressible as a useful composition of built-in Cartesian, polar, or\ngeographic marks."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use a custom renderer when the same chart scene needs a different mounted\nsurface. Use a custom SVG serializer when only SVG markup or resources differ."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Neither extension should reach into private scene compiler state."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"start-with-composition","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Start with composition"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Before creating a mark, check whether the result is a combination of:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"lines or areas;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"rectangles or cells;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"dots or hexagons;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"rules, links, ticks, arrows, or vectors;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"text or frames;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"facets;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"polar arcs, radial paths, dots, or guides;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"projected GeoJSON;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"optional scalar contours, density contours, spatial bins, topology marks,\nhierarchy layouts, or Sankey flow composition."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Composition retains built-in type inference, focus metadata, animation, and\nsubpath bundle boundaries. The "},{"type":"link","href":"../examples/index.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"chart examples"}]},{"type":"text","value":" show\ncandlesticks, networks, and annotations built this way. Boxplots use the\nfirst-party "},{"type":"link","href":"../reference/marks/box.md","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"boxX"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"boxY"},{"type":"text","value":" marks"}]},{"type":"text","value":", which keep\ntheir statistical steps aligned instead of exposing prepared child datasets."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For example, a regular scalar grid belongs in the optional\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../reference/marks/contour.md","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"contour"},{"type":"text","value":" mark"}]},{"type":"text","value":", not a case-owned D3 path and\nscene-node loop. A weighted directed graph belongs in\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../reference/marks/sankey.md","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sankeyDiagram"}]},{"type":"text","value":", whose callback composes\nordinary links, rectangles, and labels after responsive layout."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"group-reusable-child-marks","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Group reusable child marks"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"compositeMark"},{"type":"text","value":" when a reusable unit consists entirely of ordinary marks:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { compositeMark, dot, frame } from '@tanstack/charts'\n\nconst framedPoints = compositeMark(\n  [\n    frame({ id: 'border', strokeOpacity: 0.2 }),\n    dot(rows, {\n      id: 'points',\n      x: 'date',\n      y: 'value',\n      key: 'id',\n    }),\n  ],\n  { id: 'framed-points' },\n)","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The parent namespaces child channels, scene keys, points, and motion. Child\ndeclaration order remains paint order, and datum and positional types are the\nunion of the children. Parent motion supplies defaults; a child's motion wins\nwhere both specify the same field."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Child IDs must be unique. Every child retains its own interaction points, so\ndo not layer several interactive marks when only one semantic target should\nexist. A child with its own "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"resolveLayout"},{"type":"text","value":" is rejected because nested layout\nscheduling would be ambiguous. Compose those marks directly in the chart or\nwrite one custom mark with a single resolved-layout owner."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"compositeMark"},{"type":"text","value":" is available from the root and universal entries. Import\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/charts/mark/composite"},{"type":"text","value":" when bundle isolation matters."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"create-a-mark","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Create a mark"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createMark"},{"type":"text","value":" is the normal extension boundary:"}]},{"type":"html","value":"<!-- docs-example: custom-mark typecheck -->"},{"type":"code","value":"import { createMark } from '@tanstack/charts'\n\ninterface ThresholdDatum {\n  id: string\n  value: number\n}\n\nconst threshold = createMark<ThresholdDatum, never, number>(({ markIndex }) => {\n  const id = `threshold-${markIndex}`\n  const datum: ThresholdDatum = { id: 'target', value: 75 }\n\n  return {\n    id,\n    channels: {\n      y: {\n        scale: 'y',\n        values: [datum.value],\n      },\n    },\n    render({ chart, scales, theme }) {\n      const y = scales.y.map(datum.value)\n      return {\n        nodes: [\n          {\n            kind: 'rule',\n            key: datum.id,\n            x1: chart.x,\n            x2: chart.x + chart.width,\n            y1: y,\n            y2: y,\n            style: {\n              stroke: theme.foreground,\n              strokeOpacity: 0.55,\n            },\n          },\n        ],\n      }\n    },\n  }\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initialize"},{"type":"text","value":" materializes channels for one scene build. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"render"},{"type":"text","value":" receives the\nrequired full "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"surface"},{"type":"text","value":" bounds, inner "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chart"},{"type":"text","value":" plot bounds, scales, theme, color\nresolver, and text layout tools."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass a mark renderer as the third argument when the custom mark should select\nits own surface:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { canvasChartRenderer } from '@tanstack/charts/canvas'\n\nconst denseThresholds = createMark<ThresholdDatum, never, number>(\n  initializeThresholds,\n  undefined,\n  canvasChartRenderer,\n)","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The second argument remains the optional mark motion definition. A custom mark\ncan pass both motion and a renderer, and neither changes the other's meaning.\nThe selected renderer still decides whether it consumes that motion policy.\nBuilt-in marks expose the same renderer choice in their option object. A DOM\nrenderer used this way must implement "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartLayerRenderer"},{"type":"text","value":", including\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"compose(defaultRenderer)"},{"type":"text","value":", so one compositor can own the ordered child\nsurfaces. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"canvasChartRenderer"},{"type":"text","value":" provides that composition."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When a custom mark emits data labels, an optional "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"layoutLabels(context)"},{"type":"text","value":" can\nreturn those positioned "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"SceneLabel"},{"type":"text","value":" nodes before render so unlocked margins\ncontain them. Keep that method pure because responsive layout may call it more\nthan once; the final "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"render"},{"type":"text","value":" call still happens once."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For layout that genuinely requires final scales or plot bounds, return\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"resolveLayout(context)"},{"type":"text","value":" instead of an initial render. It may derive\nscreen-space bins, collisions, or topology and returns the final channels,\nlabels, states, and render closure. Positional domains still come from the\nchannels materialized by "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initialize"},{"type":"text","value":"; resolved channels can contribute to\ncolor inference. The bounded margin solver may call the layout repeatedly, so\nit must be pure and deterministic."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Available scene nodes:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"group"},{"type":"text","value":";"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"rule"},{"type":"text","value":";"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"polyline"},{"type":"text","value":";"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"area"},{"type":"text","value":";"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"dot"},{"type":"text","value":";"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"rect"},{"type":"text","value":";"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"label"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Every node requires a deterministic key."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"interaction-points","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Interaction points"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The threshold above is decorative, so it emits no points. Return "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartPoint"},{"type":"text","value":"\nrecords when custom geometry should participate in focus, tooltips, keyboard\nnavigation, or selection."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Each point should retain:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"its original datum;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"a stable key;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"semantic x and y values;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"resolved pixel coordinates;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"group identity and color."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For a large painted mark, create the semantic point once and attach that same\nobject to the scene primitive that paints it:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import type { SceneRect } from '@tanstack/charts'\n\nconst point = interactionPoint(index)\nconst node: SceneRect = {\n  kind: 'rect',\n  key: point.key,\n  x,\n  y,\n  width,\n  height,\n  interaction: { point, affinity: 'x' },\n}\n\nreturn { nodes: [node], points: [point] }","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For custom rectangle renderers, preserve "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"SceneRect.radius"},{"type":"text","value":" as the uniform\nradius and consume "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"SceneRect.cornerRadii"},{"type":"text","value":" in physical top-left, top-right,\nbottom-right, bottom-left order. Selective corners need equivalent path or\nnative geometry in every renderer the mark supports. Import\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"resolveRectCornerRadii"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"rectCornerRadiiPath"},{"type":"text","value":" from\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/charts/renderer/rect"},{"type":"text","value":" to share the built-in normalization and path\nserialization."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x"},{"type":"text","value":" for vertically oriented marks, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"y"},{"type":"text","value":" for horizontal marks, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"xy"},{"type":"text","value":" for\nordinary two-dimensional proximity, and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"geometry"},{"type":"text","value":" when only exact\ncontainment should focus the mark. The default resolver checks containment\nacross every mark before applying any fallback. A continuous "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"polyline"},{"type":"text","value":" or\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"area"},{"type":"text","value":" may attach all of the semantic samples it represents with\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"interaction: { points, affinity }"},{"type":"text","value":"; containment selects the closest sample\nwithin that primitive."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Keep primitive coordinates local when returning translated groups. Scene\ntraversal applies nested translation, clipping, facets, and paint order after\nlayout. Do not calculate a second set of global hit bounds beside the rendered\nnode."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Omit points for decorative geometry. Do not invent fake interactive data for a\nframe, grid, or threshold that should not receive focus."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"focus-only-anchors","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Focus-only anchors"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A decorative mark can still support "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"whenFocused"},{"type":"text","value":" without becoming a pointer\ntarget. Return "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"focusAnchors"},{"type":"text","value":" beside its nodes:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"return {\n  nodes: [node],\n  focusAnchors: [\n    {\n      key: node.key,\n      markId: id,\n      group: null,\n      datum,\n      datumIndex: index,\n      yValue: datum.value,\n    },\n  ],\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The anchor key must identify the node or keyed group it reveals. Include only\nthe semantic axes the geometry owns: a horizontal rule supplies "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"yValue"},{"type":"text","value":"; a\nvertical rule supplies "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"xValue"},{"type":"text","value":". "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"focusAnchors"},{"type":"text","value":" are read only when the mark is\nwrapped in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"whenFocused"},{"type":"text","value":" and never enter pointer hit testing, tooltip data, or\nkeyboard navigation."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"focus-guide-marks","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Focus-guide marks"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A mark that emits only cursor-driven rules, bands, labels, or markers declares that\nrole explicitly:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { resolveCrosshairGuide } from '@tanstack/charts/crosshair'\n\nreturn {\n  id,\n  channels: {},\n  focusGuideOnly: true,\n  render({ chart, surface, scales, theme }) {\n    return {\n      nodes: [],\n      focusGuides: [\n        {\n          key: id,\n          markId: id,\n          chart,\n          surface,\n          x: { style: { stroke: theme.foreground } },\n          projectX: (value) => {\n            const scale = scales.x\n            if (!scale || scale.type === 'none') return undefined\n            const position = (scale.viewport?.map ?? scale.map)(value)\n            return Number.isFinite(position) ? position : undefined\n          },\n          resolve: resolveCrosshairGuide,\n        },\n      ],\n    }\n  },\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"focusGuideOnly: true"},{"type":"text","value":" keeps a guide-only mark from becoming the first ordinary\nmark used to divide underlays from overlays. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"MarkScene.focusGuides"},{"type":"text","value":" accepts\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"MarkFocusGuide"},{"type":"text","value":". Its "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"placement"},{"type":"text","value":" is optional: omit it for normal mark-order\nplacement. An explicit "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"under"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"over"},{"type":"text","value":" is reserved for composed nested scenes\nthat must retain placement already resolved inside that composition. Authors\ndo not need to invent a placement for an ordinary guide mark."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The required "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"surface"},{"type":"text","value":" bounds cover the complete chart surface; "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chart"},{"type":"text","value":" covers\nthe inner plot. Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chart"},{"type":"text","value":" for clipped rules and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"surface"},{"type":"text","value":" for labels that must\nremain visible. Project semantic guide values through\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"scale.viewport?.map ?? scale.map"},{"type":"text","value":" so a transient viewport translation keeps\nthe guide aligned with presented content. Each guide's required "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"resolve"},{"type":"text","value":"\ncallback receives the final guide, local focus, pointer, and projected cursor,\nthen returns one transient scene node or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"undefined"},{"type":"text","value":". "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"resolveCrosshairGuide"},{"type":"text","value":"\nprovides the built-in rule, band, label, and marker behavior. A custom guide can\nsupply different policy without adding it to renderer bundles that never use\nthe guide. A custom renderer receives final "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"SceneFocusGuide"},{"type":"text","value":" values after the\ncompiler has filled in placement. Pass the scene to\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"resolveFocusPresentation"},{"type":"text","value":" instead of calling guide resolvers or resolving mark\norder inside the renderer."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"separate-point-and-scale-values","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Separate point and scale values"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Most marks use the same value type for interaction and scale domains. When\nthey intentionally differ, import the advanced factory:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { createMarkWithScaleValues } from '@tanstack/charts/mark/scale-values'\n\ncreateMarkWithScaleValues<Datum, PointX, PointY, ScaleX, ScaleY>(initialize)","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This is useful for interval endpoints or custom layouts whose interactive\nanchor is not the complete set of values materialized on an axis."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartMarkPointX"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartMarkPointY"},{"type":"text","value":" for the interaction contract and\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartMarkScaleX"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartMarkScaleY"},{"type":"text","value":" for the positional contract. Ordinary\nchart code should rely on definition inference instead."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"custom-scales-and-legends","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Custom scales and legends"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Configured callable scales are the normal path. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartScale"},{"type":"text","value":",\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartColorScale"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartColorLegend"},{"type":"text","value":" exist for context-aware adapters that\nneed chart range, theme, or responsive legend geometry."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Keep specialized scale dependencies in the module that uses them. A line-only\nbundle must not pay for a custom scale registered elsewhere."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"See "},{"type":"link","href":"../concepts/scales-and-d3.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Scales"}]},{"type":"text","value":" and\n"},{"type":"link","href":"./legends-and-color.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Legends and Color"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"custom-renderer","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Custom renderer"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A full renderer implements "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartRenderer"},{"type":"text","value":" and returns a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartSurface"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { mountChartRenderer } from '@tanstack/charts/renderer'\n\nconst host = mountChartRenderer(container, {\n  definition,\n  renderer: myRenderer,\n  ariaLabel: 'Threshold history',\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The renderer owns server shell markup, its mounted element, scene painting,\nfocus painting, and cleanup. It can implement "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"clientToScene"},{"type":"text","value":" when controlled\npointer gestures need client-coordinate conversion; the interaction controller\nreturns "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"null"},{"type":"text","value":" when that optional capability is absent. The host retains\nsizing, runtime, keyboard, tooltip, selection, and focus-strategy behavior.\nKeep "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"prerender"},{"type":"text","value":" deterministic and make "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"mount"},{"type":"text","value":" adopt compatible server markup."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A composed surface exposes its child surfaces from back to front through\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartSurface.layers"},{"type":"text","value":". Its "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"element"},{"type":"text","value":" remains the single accessible,\ninteractive root. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"defaultElement"},{"type":"text","value":" identifies the topmost element owned by the\nhost's default renderer. SVG-oriented "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onRender"},{"type":"text","value":" callbacks keep "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"svg"},{"type":"text","value":" for\ncompatibility and also receive the complete "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"surface"},{"type":"text","value":", so application code can\ninspect a mixed chart without treating the composition root as an SVG."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"paintFocus"},{"type":"text","value":" resolves and paints inline mark-state geometry, return that\ndestination "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartScene"},{"type":"text","value":". The host will use it for subsequent pointer hits;\nreturning nothing preserves base-scene interaction for simpler renderers.\nCall "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"resolveFocusPresentation(scene, focus, pointer, cursor)"},{"type":"text","value":" to obtain the\nauthored and crosshair nodes for the renderer's underlay and overlay surfaces."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If the renderer animates point geometry, implement "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getPresentationPoints"},{"type":"text","value":"\nand "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"subscribePresentationPoints"},{"type":"text","value":". This keeps stationary pointer focus,\nkeyboard focus, and tooltip anchors aligned with the painted frame."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use the public "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"resolveFocusScene"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"focusedSceneNodes"},{"type":"text","value":" helpers when a\ncustom surface supports authored focus layers. They keep normal filtered marks\nand "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"whenFocused(..., { retarget: true })"},{"type":"text","value":" compositions on the same\nrenderer-neutral selection path as the built-in surfaces."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartRendererRenderContext.surface"},{"type":"text","value":" instead of assuming "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onRender"},{"type":"text","value":" exposes\nan SVG element. Framework consumers pass "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"renderer"},{"type":"text","value":" through\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/charts/react/core"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/charts/octane/core"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"custom-svg-serializer","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Custom SVG serializer"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartSvgRenderer"},{"type":"text","value":" accepts the complete "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartScene"},{"type":"text","value":" and accessible SVG\noptions:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const renderSvg: ChartSvgRenderer<Row, Date, number> = (scene, options) => {\n  return serializeMySvg(scene, options)\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass it through "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"renderSvg"},{"type":"text","value":" on the vanilla host or any default SVG framework\nadapter. Preserve:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"the accessible label and description;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"stable "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data-ts-key"},{"type":"text","value":" identity when DOM reconciliation should reuse nodes;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"the focus marker contract when chart-owned focus remains enabled;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"scoped IDs through "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"idPrefix"},{"type":"text","value":";"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"deterministic server output."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The default "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"renderChartSvg"},{"type":"text","value":" already emits declared gradients and group clips.\nThe compatible "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"renderChartSvgWithResources"},{"type":"text","value":" export remains available when an\nexplicit resource serializer name is useful."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Mounted SVG surfaces also call the selected serializer when focus guides are\npainted. That call contains a single "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"focus-guide-layer:under"},{"type":"text","value":" or\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"focus-guide-layer:over"},{"type":"text","value":" group in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"scene.nodes"},{"type":"text","value":"; preserve its keyed "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"<g>"},{"type":"text","value":" and\napply the same paint, clipping, and resource-ID rules as the base scene."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"custom-focus-and-spatial-indexes","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Custom focus and spatial indexes"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartFocusStrategy"},{"type":"text","value":" owns pointer resolution, grouping, and keyboard\nnavigation. Pointer coordinates and the point being grouped arrive through\nthe second context bag. Its generic types must remain identical to the chart\npoints it receives."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartSpatialIndexFactory"},{"type":"text","value":" builds optional nearest-point acceleration from\nscene points and receives the complete resolved scene through\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"context.scene"},{"type":"text","value":". Return original typed points from the index. Do not erase\nthem to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"unknown"},{"type":"text","value":" and cast them back in callbacks."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"extension-checklist","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Extension checklist"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Built-in composition was considered first."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Channel values fully declare positional domain inputs."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Scene generation is deterministic and DOM-free."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Keys survive reorder and updates."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Interactive points retain original data and exact coordinate types."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Decorative geometry emits no fake points."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Optional dependencies remain behind the extension import."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Custom rendering preserves accessibility, identity, and SSR."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"No cast, private import, or suppression is needed at the public boundary."}]}]}]}],"headings":[{"id":"start-with-composition","text":"Start with composition","level":2},{"id":"group-reusable-child-marks","text":"Group reusable child marks","level":2},{"id":"create-a-mark","text":"Create a mark","level":2},{"id":"interaction-points","text":"Interaction points","level":2},{"id":"focus-only-anchors","text":"Focus-only anchors","level":2},{"id":"focus-guide-marks","text":"Focus-guide marks","level":2},{"id":"separate-point-and-scale-values","text":"Separate point and scale values","level":2},{"id":"custom-scales-and-legends","text":"Custom scales and legends","level":2},{"id":"custom-renderer","text":"Custom renderer","level":2},{"id":"custom-svg-serializer","text":"Custom SVG serializer","level":2},{"id":"custom-focus-and-spatial-indexes","text":"Custom focus and spatial indexes","level":2},{"id":"extension-checklist","text":"Extension checklist","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nUse a custom mark when a visualization fits the shared scene model but is not\nexpressible as a useful composition of built-in Cartesian, polar, or\ngeographic marks.\n\nUse a custom renderer when the same chart scene needs a different mounted\nsurface. Use a custom SVG serializer when only SVG markup or resources differ.\n\nNeither extension should reach into private scene compiler state.\n\n## Start with composition\n\nBefore creating a mark, check whether the result is a combination of:\n\n- lines or areas;\n- rectangles or cells;\n- dots or hexagons;\n- rules, links, ticks, arrows, or vectors;\n- text or frames;\n- facets;\n- polar arcs, radial paths, dots, or guides;\n- projected GeoJSON;\n- optional scalar contours, density contours, spatial bins, topology marks,\n  hierarchy layouts, or Sankey flow composition.\n\nComposition retains built-in type inference, focus metadata, animation, and\nsubpath bundle boundaries. The [chart examples](../examples/index.md) show\ncandlesticks, networks, and annotations built this way. Boxplots use the\nfirst-party [`boxX` and `boxY` marks](../reference/marks/box.md), which keep\ntheir statistical steps aligned instead of exposing prepared child datasets.\n\nFor example, a regular scalar grid belongs in the optional\n[`contour` mark](../reference/marks/contour.md), not a case-owned D3 path and\nscene-node loop. A weighted directed graph belongs in\n[`sankeyDiagram`](../reference/marks/sankey.md), whose callback composes\nordinary links, rectangles, and labels after responsive layout.\n\n## Group reusable child marks\n\nUse `compositeMark` when a reusable unit consists entirely of ordinary marks:\n\n```ts\nimport { compositeMark, dot, frame } from '@tanstack/charts'\n\nconst framedPoints = compositeMark(\n  [\n    frame({ id: 'border', strokeOpacity: 0.2 }),\n    dot(rows, {\n      id: 'points',\n      x: 'date',\n      y: 'value',\n      key: 'id',\n    }),\n  ],\n  { id: 'framed-points' },\n)\n```\n\nThe parent namespaces child channels, scene keys, points, and motion. Child\ndeclaration order remains paint order, and datum and positional types are the\nunion of the children. Parent motion supplies defaults; a child's motion wins\nwhere both specify the same field.\n\nChild IDs must be unique. Every child retains its own interaction points, so\ndo not layer several interactive marks when only one semantic target should\nexist. A child with its own `resolveLayout` is rejected because nested layout\nscheduling would be ambiguous. Compose those marks directly in the chart or\nwrite one custom mark with a single resolved-layout owner.\n\n`compositeMark` is available from the root and universal entries. Import\n`@tanstack/charts/mark/composite` when bundle isolation matters.\n\n## Create a mark\n\n`createMark` is the normal extension boundary:\n\n<!-- docs-example: custom-mark typecheck -->\n\n```ts\nimport { createMark } from '@tanstack/charts'\n\ninterface ThresholdDatum {\n  id: string\n  value: number\n}\n\nconst threshold = createMark<ThresholdDatum, never, number>(({ markIndex }) => {\n  const id = `threshold-${markIndex}`\n  const datum: ThresholdDatum = { id: 'target', value: 75 }\n\n  return {\n    id,\n    channels: {\n      y: {\n        scale: 'y',\n        values: [datum.value],\n      },\n    },\n    render({ chart, scales, theme }) {\n      const y = scales.y.map(datum.value)\n      return {\n        nodes: [\n          {\n            kind: 'rule',\n            key: datum.id,\n            x1: chart.x,\n            x2: chart.x + chart.width,\n            y1: y,\n            y2: y,\n            style: {\n              stroke: theme.foreground,\n              strokeOpacity: 0.55,\n            },\n          },\n        ],\n      }\n    },\n  }\n})\n```\n\n`initialize` materializes channels for one scene build. `render` receives the\nrequired full `surface` bounds, inner `chart` plot bounds, scales, theme, color\nresolver, and text layout tools.\n\nPass a mark renderer as the third argument when the custom mark should select\nits own surface:\n\n```ts\nimport { canvasChartRenderer } from '@tanstack/charts/canvas'\n\nconst denseThresholds = createMark<ThresholdDatum, never, number>(\n  initializeThresholds,\n  undefined,\n  canvasChartRenderer,\n)\n```\n\nThe second argument remains the optional mark motion definition. A custom mark\ncan pass both motion and a renderer, and neither changes the other's meaning.\nThe selected renderer still decides whether it consumes that motion policy.\nBuilt-in marks expose the same renderer choice in their option object. A DOM\nrenderer used this way must implement `ChartLayerRenderer`, including\n`compose(defaultRenderer)`, so one compositor can own the ordered child\nsurfaces. `canvasChartRenderer` provides that composition.\n\nWhen a custom mark emits data labels, an optional `layoutLabels(context)` can\nreturn those positioned `SceneLabel` nodes before render so unlocked margins\ncontain them. Keep that method pure because responsive layout may call it more\nthan once; the final `render` call still happens once.\n\nFor layout that genuinely requires final scales or plot bounds, return\n`resolveLayout(context)` instead of an initial render. It may derive\nscreen-space bins, collisions, or topology and returns the final channels,\nlabels, states, and render closure. Positional domains still come from the\nchannels materialized by `initialize`; resolved channels can contribute to\ncolor inference. The bounded margin solver may call the layout repeatedly, so\nit must be pure and deterministic.\n\nAvailable scene nodes:\n\n- `group`;\n- `rule`;\n- `polyline`;\n- `area`;\n- `dot`;\n- `rect`;\n- `label`.\n\nEvery node requires a deterministic key.\n\n## Interaction points\n\nThe threshold above is decorative, so it emits no points. Return `ChartPoint`\nrecords when custom geometry should participate in focus, tooltips, keyboard\nnavigation, or selection.\n\nEach point should retain:\n\n- its original datum;\n- a stable key;\n- semantic x and y values;\n- resolved pixel coordinates;\n- group identity and color.\n\nFor a large painted mark, create the semantic point once and attach that same\nobject to the scene primitive that paints it:\n\n```ts\nimport type { SceneRect } from '@tanstack/charts'\n\nconst point = interactionPoint(index)\nconst node: SceneRect = {\n  kind: 'rect',\n  key: point.key,\n  x,\n  y,\n  width,\n  height,\n  interaction: { point, affinity: 'x' },\n}\n\nreturn { nodes: [node], points: [point] }\n```\n\nFor custom rectangle renderers, preserve `SceneRect.radius` as the uniform\nradius and consume `SceneRect.cornerRadii` in physical top-left, top-right,\nbottom-right, bottom-left order. Selective corners need equivalent path or\nnative geometry in every renderer the mark supports. Import\n`resolveRectCornerRadii` and `rectCornerRadiiPath` from\n`@tanstack/charts/renderer/rect` to share the built-in normalization and path\nserialization.\n\nUse `x` for vertically oriented marks, `y` for horizontal marks, `xy` for\nordinary two-dimensional proximity, and `geometry` when only exact\ncontainment should focus the mark. The default resolver checks containment\nacross every mark before applying any fallback. A continuous `polyline` or\n`area` may attach all of the semantic samples it represents with\n`interaction: { points, affinity }`; containment selects the closest sample\nwithin that primitive.\n\nKeep primitive coordinates local when returning translated groups. Scene\ntraversal applies nested translation, clipping, facets, and paint order after\nlayout. Do not calculate a second set of global hit bounds beside the rendered\nnode.\n\nOmit points for decorative geometry. Do not invent fake interactive data for a\nframe, grid, or threshold that should not receive focus.\n\n## Focus-only anchors\n\nA decorative mark can still support `whenFocused` without becoming a pointer\ntarget. Return `focusAnchors` beside its nodes:\n\n```ts\nreturn {\n  nodes: [node],\n  focusAnchors: [\n    {\n      key: node.key,\n      markId: id,\n      group: null,\n      datum,\n      datumIndex: index,\n      yValue: datum.value,\n    },\n  ],\n}\n```\n\nThe anchor key must identify the node or keyed group it reveals. Include only\nthe semantic axes the geometry owns: a horizontal rule supplies `yValue`; a\nvertical rule supplies `xValue`. `focusAnchors` are read only when the mark is\nwrapped in `whenFocused` and never enter pointer hit testing, tooltip data, or\nkeyboard navigation.\n\n## Focus-guide marks\n\nA mark that emits only cursor-driven rules, bands, labels, or markers declares that\nrole explicitly:\n\n```ts\nimport { resolveCrosshairGuide } from '@tanstack/charts/crosshair'\n\nreturn {\n  id,\n  channels: {},\n  focusGuideOnly: true,\n  render({ chart, surface, scales, theme }) {\n    return {\n      nodes: [],\n      focusGuides: [\n        {\n          key: id,\n          markId: id,\n          chart,\n          surface,\n          x: { style: { stroke: theme.foreground } },\n          projectX: (value) => {\n            const scale = scales.x\n            if (!scale || scale.type === 'none') return undefined\n            const position = (scale.viewport?.map ?? scale.map)(value)\n            return Number.isFinite(position) ? position : undefined\n          },\n          resolve: resolveCrosshairGuide,\n        },\n      ],\n    }\n  },\n}\n```\n\n`focusGuideOnly: true` keeps a guide-only mark from becoming the first ordinary\nmark used to divide underlays from overlays. `MarkScene.focusGuides` accepts\n`MarkFocusGuide`. Its `placement` is optional: omit it for normal mark-order\nplacement. An explicit `under` or `over` is reserved for composed nested scenes\nthat must retain placement already resolved inside that composition. Authors\ndo not need to invent a placement for an ordinary guide mark.\n\nThe required `surface` bounds cover the complete chart surface; `chart` covers\nthe inner plot. Use `chart` for clipped rules and `surface` for labels that must\nremain visible. Project semantic guide values through\n`scale.viewport?.map ?? scale.map` so a transient viewport translation keeps\nthe guide aligned with presented content. Each guide's required `resolve`\ncallback receives the final guide, local focus, pointer, and projected cursor,\nthen returns one transient scene node or `undefined`. `resolveCrosshairGuide`\nprovides the built-in rule, band, label, and marker behavior. A custom guide can\nsupply different policy without adding it to renderer bundles that never use\nthe guide. A custom renderer receives final `SceneFocusGuide` values after the\ncompiler has filled in placement. Pass the scene to\n`resolveFocusPresentation` instead of calling guide resolvers or resolving mark\norder inside the renderer.\n\n## Separate point and scale values\n\nMost marks use the same value type for interaction and scale domains. When\nthey intentionally differ, import the advanced factory:\n\n```ts\nimport { createMarkWithScaleValues } from '@tanstack/charts/mark/scale-values'\n\ncreateMarkWithScaleValues<Datum, PointX, PointY, ScaleX, ScaleY>(initialize)\n```\n\nThis is useful for interval endpoints or custom layouts whose interactive\nanchor is not the complete set of values materialized on an axis.\n\nUse `ChartMarkPointX` and `ChartMarkPointY` for the interaction contract and\n`ChartMarkScaleX` and `ChartMarkScaleY` for the positional contract. Ordinary\nchart code should rely on definition inference instead.\n\n## Custom scales and legends\n\nConfigured callable scales are the normal path. `ChartScale`,\n`ChartColorScale`, and `ChartColorLegend` exist for context-aware adapters that\nneed chart range, theme, or responsive legend geometry.\n\nKeep specialized scale dependencies in the module that uses them. A line-only\nbundle must not pay for a custom scale registered elsewhere.\n\nSee [Scales](../concepts/scales-and-d3.md) and\n[Legends and Color](./legends-and-color.md).\n\n## Custom renderer\n\nA full renderer implements `ChartRenderer` and returns a `ChartSurface`:\n\n```ts\nimport { mountChartRenderer } from '@tanstack/charts/renderer'\n\nconst host = mountChartRenderer(container, {\n  definition,\n  renderer: myRenderer,\n  ariaLabel: 'Threshold history',\n})\n```\n\nThe renderer owns server shell markup, its mounted element, scene painting,\nfocus painting, and cleanup. It can implement `clientToScene` when controlled\npointer gestures need client-coordinate conversion; the interaction controller\nreturns `null` when that optional capability is absent. The host retains\nsizing, runtime, keyboard, tooltip, selection, and focus-strategy behavior.\nKeep `prerender` deterministic and make `mount` adopt compatible server markup.\n\nA composed surface exposes its child surfaces from back to front through\n`ChartSurface.layers`. Its `element` remains the single accessible,\ninteractive root. `defaultElement` identifies the topmost element owned by the\nhost's default renderer. SVG-oriented `onRender` callbacks keep `svg` for\ncompatibility and also receive the complete `surface`, so application code can\ninspect a mixed chart without treating the composition root as an SVG.\n\nIf `paintFocus` resolves and paints inline mark-state geometry, return that\ndestination `ChartScene`. The host will use it for subsequent pointer hits;\nreturning nothing preserves base-scene interaction for simpler renderers.\nCall `resolveFocusPresentation(scene, focus, pointer, cursor)` to obtain the\nauthored and crosshair nodes for the renderer's underlay and overlay surfaces.\n\nIf the renderer animates point geometry, implement `getPresentationPoints`\nand `subscribePresentationPoints`. This keeps stationary pointer focus,\nkeyboard focus, and tooltip anchors aligned with the painted frame.\n\nUse the public `resolveFocusScene` and `focusedSceneNodes` helpers when a\ncustom surface supports authored focus layers. They keep normal filtered marks\nand `whenFocused(..., { retarget: true })` compositions on the same\nrenderer-neutral selection path as the built-in surfaces.\n\nUse `ChartRendererRenderContext.surface` instead of assuming `onRender` exposes\nan SVG element. Framework consumers pass `renderer` through\n`@tanstack/charts/react/core` or `@tanstack/charts/octane/core`.\n\n## Custom SVG serializer\n\nA `ChartSvgRenderer` accepts the complete `ChartScene` and accessible SVG\noptions:\n\n```ts\nconst renderSvg: ChartSvgRenderer<Row, Date, number> = (scene, options) => {\n  return serializeMySvg(scene, options)\n}\n```\n\nPass it through `renderSvg` on the vanilla host or any default SVG framework\nadapter. Preserve:\n\n- the accessible label and description;\n- stable `data-ts-key` identity when DOM reconciliation should reuse nodes;\n- the focus marker contract when chart-owned focus remains enabled;\n- scoped IDs through `idPrefix`;\n- deterministic server output.\n\nThe default `renderChartSvg` already emits declared gradients and group clips.\nThe compatible `renderChartSvgWithResources` export remains available when an\nexplicit resource serializer name is useful.\n\nMounted SVG surfaces also call the selected serializer when focus guides are\npainted. That call contains a single `focus-guide-layer:under` or\n`focus-guide-layer:over` group in `scene.nodes`; preserve its keyed `<g>` and\napply the same paint, clipping, and resource-ID rules as the base scene.\n\n## Custom focus and spatial indexes\n\nA `ChartFocusStrategy` owns pointer resolution, grouping, and keyboard\nnavigation. Pointer coordinates and the point being grouped arrive through\nthe second context bag. Its generic types must remain identical to the chart\npoints it receives.\n\nA `ChartSpatialIndexFactory` builds optional nearest-point acceleration from\nscene points and receives the complete resolved scene through\n`context.scene`. Return original typed points from the index. Do not erase\nthem to `unknown` and cast them back in callbacks.\n\n## Extension checklist\n\n- Built-in composition was considered first.\n- Channel values fully declare positional domain inputs.\n- Scene generation is deterministic and DOM-free.\n- Keys survive reorder and updates.\n- Interactive points retain original data and exact coordinate types.\n- Decorative geometry emits no fake points.\n- Optional dependencies remain behind the extension import.\n- Custom rendering preserves accessibility, identity, and SSR.\n- No cast, private import, or suppression is needed at the public boundary.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Custom Marks and Renderers\ndescription: Extend the grammar with typed mark channels and keyed scene nodes, or replace the mounted renderer without bypassing chart semantics.\n---\n\nUse a custom mark when a visualization fits the shared scene model but is not\nexpressible as a useful composition of built-in Cartesian, polar, or\ngeographic marks.\n\nUse a custom renderer when the same chart scene needs a different mounted\nsurface. Use a custom SVG serializer when only SVG markup or resources differ.\n\nNeither extension should reach into private scene compiler state.\n\n## Start with composition\n\nBefore creating a mark, check whether the result is a combination of:\n\n- lines or areas;\n- rectangles or cells;\n- dots or hexagons;\n- rules, links, ticks, arrows, or vectors;\n- text or frames;\n- facets;\n- polar arcs, radial paths, dots, or guides;\n- projected GeoJSON;\n- optional scalar contours, density contours, spatial bins, topology marks,\n  hierarchy layouts, or Sankey flow composition.\n\nComposition retains built-in type inference, focus metadata, animation, and\nsubpath bundle boundaries. The [chart examples](../examples/index.md) show\ncandlesticks, networks, and annotations built this way. Boxplots use the\nfirst-party [`boxX` and `boxY` marks](../reference/marks/box.md), which keep\ntheir statistical steps aligned instead of exposing prepared child datasets.\n\nFor example, a regular scalar grid belongs in the optional\n[`contour` mark](../reference/marks/contour.md), not a case-owned D3 path and\nscene-node loop. A weighted directed graph belongs in\n[`sankeyDiagram`](../reference/marks/sankey.md), whose callback composes\nordinary links, rectangles, and labels after responsive layout.\n\n## Group reusable child marks\n\nUse `compositeMark` when a reusable unit consists entirely of ordinary marks:\n\n```ts\nimport { compositeMark, dot, frame } from '@tanstack/charts'\n\nconst framedPoints = compositeMark(\n  [\n    frame({ id: 'border', strokeOpacity: 0.2 }),\n    dot(rows, {\n      id: 'points',\n      x: 'date',\n      y: 'value',\n      key: 'id',\n    }),\n  ],\n  { id: 'framed-points' },\n)\n```\n\nThe parent namespaces child channels, scene keys, points, and motion. Child\ndeclaration order remains paint order, and datum and positional types are the\nunion of the children. Parent motion supplies defaults; a child's motion wins\nwhere both specify the same field.\n\nChild IDs must be unique. Every child retains its own interaction points, so\ndo not layer several interactive marks when only one semantic target should\nexist. A child with its own `resolveLayout` is rejected because nested layout\nscheduling would be ambiguous. Compose those marks directly in the chart or\nwrite one custom mark with a single resolved-layout owner.\n\n`compositeMark` is available from the root and universal entries. Import\n`@tanstack/charts/mark/composite` when bundle isolation matters.\n\n## Create a mark\n\n`createMark` is the normal extension boundary:\n\n<!-- docs-example: custom-mark typecheck -->\n\n```ts\nimport { createMark } from '@tanstack/charts'\n\ninterface ThresholdDatum {\n  id: string\n  value: number\n}\n\nconst threshold = createMark<ThresholdDatum, never, number>(({ markIndex }) => {\n  const id = `threshold-${markIndex}`\n  const datum: ThresholdDatum = { id: 'target', value: 75 }\n\n  return {\n    id,\n    channels: {\n      y: {\n        scale: 'y',\n        values: [datum.value],\n      },\n    },\n    render({ chart, scales, theme }) {\n      const y = scales.y.map(datum.value)\n      return {\n        nodes: [\n          {\n            kind: 'rule',\n            key: datum.id,\n            x1: chart.x,\n            x2: chart.x + chart.width,\n            y1: y,\n            y2: y,\n            style: {\n              stroke: theme.foreground,\n              strokeOpacity: 0.55,\n            },\n          },\n        ],\n      }\n    },\n  }\n})\n```\n\n`initialize` materializes channels for one scene build. `render` receives the\nrequired full `surface` bounds, inner `chart` plot bounds, scales, theme, color\nresolver, and text layout tools.\n\nPass a mark renderer as the third argument when the custom mark should select\nits own surface:\n\n```ts\nimport { canvasChartRenderer } from '@tanstack/charts/canvas'\n\nconst denseThresholds = createMark<ThresholdDatum, never, number>(\n  initializeThresholds,\n  undefined,\n  canvasChartRenderer,\n)\n```\n\nThe second argument remains the optional mark motion definition. A custom mark\ncan pass both motion and a renderer, and neither changes the other's meaning.\nThe selected renderer still decides whether it consumes that motion policy.\nBuilt-in marks expose the same renderer choice in their option object. A DOM\nrenderer used this way must implement `ChartLayerRenderer`, including\n`compose(defaultRenderer)`, so one compositor can own the ordered child\nsurfaces. `canvasChartRenderer` provides that composition.\n\nWhen a custom mark emits data labels, an optional `layoutLabels(context)` can\nreturn those positioned `SceneLabel` nodes before render so unlocked margins\ncontain them. Keep that method pure because responsive layout may call it more\nthan once; the final `render` call still happens once.\n\nFor layout that genuinely requires final scales or plot bounds, return\n`resolveLayout(context)` instead of an initial render. It may derive\nscreen-space bins, collisions, or topology and returns the final channels,\nlabels, states, and render closure. Positional domains still come from the\nchannels materialized by `initialize`; resolved channels can contribute to\ncolor inference. The bounded margin solver may call the layout repeatedly, so\nit must be pure and deterministic.\n\nAvailable scene nodes:\n\n- `group`;\n- `rule`;\n- `polyline`;\n- `area`;\n- `dot`;\n- `rect`;\n- `label`.\n\nEvery node requires a deterministic key.\n\n## Interaction points\n\nThe threshold above is decorative, so it emits no points. Return `ChartPoint`\nrecords when custom geometry should participate in focus, tooltips, keyboard\nnavigation, or selection.\n\nEach point should retain:\n\n- its original datum;\n- a stable key;\n- semantic x and y values;\n- resolved pixel coordinates;\n- group identity and color.\n\nFor a large painted mark, create the semantic point once and attach that same\nobject to the scene primitive that paints it:\n\n```ts\nimport type { SceneRect } from '@tanstack/charts'\n\nconst point = interactionPoint(index)\nconst node: SceneRect = {\n  kind: 'rect',\n  key: point.key,\n  x,\n  y,\n  width,\n  height,\n  interaction: { point, affinity: 'x' },\n}\n\nreturn { nodes: [node], points: [point] }\n```\n\nFor custom rectangle renderers, preserve `SceneRect.radius` as the uniform\nradius and consume `SceneRect.cornerRadii` in physical top-left, top-right,\nbottom-right, bottom-left order. Selective corners need equivalent path or\nnative geometry in every renderer the mark supports. Import\n`resolveRectCornerRadii` and `rectCornerRadiiPath` from\n`@tanstack/charts/renderer/rect` to share the built-in normalization and path\nserialization.\n\nUse `x` for vertically oriented marks, `y` for horizontal marks, `xy` for\nordinary two-dimensional proximity, and `geometry` when only exact\ncontainment should focus the mark. The default resolver checks containment\nacross every mark before applying any fallback. A continuous `polyline` or\n`area` may attach all of the semantic samples it represents with\n`interaction: { points, affinity }`; containment selects the closest sample\nwithin that primitive.\n\nKeep primitive coordinates local when returning translated groups. Scene\ntraversal applies nested translation, clipping, facets, and paint order after\nlayout. Do not calculate a second set of global hit bounds beside the rendered\nnode.\n\nOmit points for decorative geometry. Do not invent fake interactive data for a\nframe, grid, or threshold that should not receive focus.\n\n## Focus-only anchors\n\nA decorative mark can still support `whenFocused` without becoming a pointer\ntarget. Return `focusAnchors` beside its nodes:\n\n```ts\nreturn {\n  nodes: [node],\n  focusAnchors: [\n    {\n      key: node.key,\n      markId: id,\n      group: null,\n      datum,\n      datumIndex: index,\n      yValue: datum.value,\n    },\n  ],\n}\n```\n\nThe anchor key must identify the node or keyed group it reveals. Include only\nthe semantic axes the geometry owns: a horizontal rule supplies `yValue`; a\nvertical rule supplies `xValue`. `focusAnchors` are read only when the mark is\nwrapped in `whenFocused` and never enter pointer hit testing, tooltip data, or\nkeyboard navigation.\n\n## Focus-guide marks\n\nA mark that emits only cursor-driven rules, bands, labels, or markers declares that\nrole explicitly:\n\n```ts\nimport { resolveCrosshairGuide } from '@tanstack/charts/crosshair'\n\nreturn {\n  id,\n  channels: {},\n  focusGuideOnly: true,\n  render({ chart, surface, scales, theme }) {\n    return {\n      nodes: [],\n      focusGuides: [\n        {\n          key: id,\n          markId: id,\n          chart,\n          surface,\n          x: { style: { stroke: theme.foreground } },\n          projectX: (value) => {\n            const scale = scales.x\n            if (!scale || scale.type === 'none') return undefined\n            const position = (scale.viewport?.map ?? scale.map)(value)\n            return Number.isFinite(position) ? position : undefined\n          },\n          resolve: resolveCrosshairGuide,\n        },\n      ],\n    }\n  },\n}\n```\n\n`focusGuideOnly: true` keeps a guide-only mark from becoming the first ordinary\nmark used to divide underlays from overlays. `MarkScene.focusGuides` accepts\n`MarkFocusGuide`. Its `placement` is optional: omit it for normal mark-order\nplacement. An explicit `under` or `over` is reserved for composed nested scenes\nthat must retain placement already resolved inside that composition. Authors\ndo not need to invent a placement for an ordinary guide mark.\n\nThe required `surface` bounds cover the complete chart surface; `chart` covers\nthe inner plot. Use `chart` for clipped rules and `surface` for labels that must\nremain visible. Project semantic guide values through\n`scale.viewport?.map ?? scale.map` so a transient viewport translation keeps\nthe guide aligned with presented content. Each guide's required `resolve`\ncallback receives the final guide, local focus, pointer, and projected cursor,\nthen returns one transient scene node or `undefined`. `resolveCrosshairGuide`\nprovides the built-in rule, band, label, and marker behavior. A custom guide can\nsupply different policy without adding it to renderer bundles that never use\nthe guide. A custom renderer receives final `SceneFocusGuide` values after the\ncompiler has filled in placement. Pass the scene to\n`resolveFocusPresentation` instead of calling guide resolvers or resolving mark\norder inside the renderer.\n\n## Separate point and scale values\n\nMost marks use the same value type for interaction and scale domains. When\nthey intentionally differ, import the advanced factory:\n\n```ts\nimport { createMarkWithScaleValues } from '@tanstack/charts/mark/scale-values'\n\ncreateMarkWithScaleValues<Datum, PointX, PointY, ScaleX, ScaleY>(initialize)\n```\n\nThis is useful for interval endpoints or custom layouts whose interactive\nanchor is not the complete set of values materialized on an axis.\n\nUse `ChartMarkPointX` and `ChartMarkPointY` for the interaction contract and\n`ChartMarkScaleX` and `ChartMarkScaleY` for the positional contract. Ordinary\nchart code should rely on definition inference instead.\n\n## Custom scales and legends\n\nConfigured callable scales are the normal path. `ChartScale`,\n`ChartColorScale`, and `ChartColorLegend` exist for context-aware adapters that\nneed chart range, theme, or responsive legend geometry.\n\nKeep specialized scale dependencies in the module that uses them. A line-only\nbundle must not pay for a custom scale registered elsewhere.\n\nSee [Scales](../concepts/scales-and-d3.md) and\n[Legends and Color](./legends-and-color.md).\n\n## Custom renderer\n\nA full renderer implements `ChartRenderer` and returns a `ChartSurface`:\n\n```ts\nimport { mountChartRenderer } from '@tanstack/charts/renderer'\n\nconst host = mountChartRenderer(container, {\n  definition,\n  renderer: myRenderer,\n  ariaLabel: 'Threshold history',\n})\n```\n\nThe renderer owns server shell markup, its mounted element, scene painting,\nfocus painting, and cleanup. It can implement `clientToScene` when controlled\npointer gestures need client-coordinate conversion; the interaction controller\nreturns `null` when that optional capability is absent. The host retains\nsizing, runtime, keyboard, tooltip, selection, and focus-strategy behavior.\nKeep `prerender` deterministic and make `mount` adopt compatible server markup.\n\nA composed surface exposes its child surfaces from back to front through\n`ChartSurface.layers`. Its `element` remains the single accessible,\ninteractive root. `defaultElement` identifies the topmost element owned by the\nhost's default renderer. SVG-oriented `onRender` callbacks keep `svg` for\ncompatibility and also receive the complete `surface`, so application code can\ninspect a mixed chart without treating the composition root as an SVG.\n\nIf `paintFocus` resolves and paints inline mark-state geometry, return that\ndestination `ChartScene`. The host will use it for subsequent pointer hits;\nreturning nothing preserves base-scene interaction for simpler renderers.\nCall `resolveFocusPresentation(scene, focus, pointer, cursor)` to obtain the\nauthored and crosshair nodes for the renderer's underlay and overlay surfaces.\n\nIf the renderer animates point geometry, implement `getPresentationPoints`\nand `subscribePresentationPoints`. This keeps stationary pointer focus,\nkeyboard focus, and tooltip anchors aligned with the painted frame.\n\nUse the public `resolveFocusScene` and `focusedSceneNodes` helpers when a\ncustom surface supports authored focus layers. They keep normal filtered marks\nand `whenFocused(..., { retarget: true })` compositions on the same\nrenderer-neutral selection path as the built-in surfaces.\n\nUse `ChartRendererRenderContext.surface` instead of assuming `onRender` exposes\nan SVG element. Framework consumers pass `renderer` through\n`@tanstack/charts/react/core` or `@tanstack/charts/octane/core`.\n\n## Custom SVG serializer\n\nA `ChartSvgRenderer` accepts the complete `ChartScene` and accessible SVG\noptions:\n\n```ts\nconst renderSvg: ChartSvgRenderer<Row, Date, number> = (scene, options) => {\n  return serializeMySvg(scene, options)\n}\n```\n\nPass it through `renderSvg` on the vanilla host or any default SVG framework\nadapter. Preserve:\n\n- the accessible label and description;\n- stable `data-ts-key` identity when DOM reconciliation should reuse nodes;\n- the focus marker contract when chart-owned focus remains enabled;\n- scoped IDs through `idPrefix`;\n- deterministic server output.\n\nThe default `renderChartSvg` already emits declared gradients and group clips.\nThe compatible `renderChartSvgWithResources` export remains available when an\nexplicit resource serializer name is useful.\n\nMounted SVG surfaces also call the selected serializer when focus guides are\npainted. That call contains a single `focus-guide-layer:under` or\n`focus-guide-layer:over` group in `scene.nodes`; preserve its keyed `<g>` and\napply the same paint, clipping, and resource-ID rules as the base scene.\n\n## Custom focus and spatial indexes\n\nA `ChartFocusStrategy` owns pointer resolution, grouping, and keyboard\nnavigation. Pointer coordinates and the point being grouped arrive through\nthe second context bag. Its generic types must remain identical to the chart\npoints it receives.\n\nA `ChartSpatialIndexFactory` builds optional nearest-point acceleration from\nscene points and receives the complete resolved scene through\n`context.scene`. Return original typed points from the index. Do not erase\nthem to `unknown` and cast them back in callbacks.\n\n## Extension checklist\n\n- Built-in composition was considered first.\n- Channel values fully declare positional domain inputs.\n- Scene generation is deterministic and DOM-free.\n- Keys survive reorder and updates.\n- Interactive points retain original data and exact coordinate types.\n- Decorative geometry emits no fake points.\n- Optional dependencies remain behind the extension import.\n- Custom rendering preserves accessibility, identity, and SSR.\n- No cast, private import, or suppression is needed at the public boundary.\n"}
